==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈdʒɑː.ɡən/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒɑɹ.ɡən/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-jargon.ogg|Audio (GA)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)ɡən|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|jar|gon}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|jargoun}}, {{m|enm|jargon}}, from {{uder|en|fro|jargon}}, a variant of {{m|fro|gargon}}, {{m|fro|gargun||chatter; talk; language}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[technical]] [[terminology]] [[unique]] to a [[particular]] [[subject#Noun|subject]].
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[language]] [[characteristic#Adjective|characteristic]] of a particular [[group#Noun|group]].
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=I|chapter=I|page=11|passage=They [the Normans] abandoned their native speech, and adopted the French tongue, in which Latin was the predominant element. They speedily raised their new language to a dignity and importance which it had never before possessed. They found it a barbarous '''jargon'''; they fixed it in writing; and they employed it in legislation, in poetry, and in romance.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ian Hodder|title=Archaeological Theory Today|year=2014|passage=In fact all the competing theories have developed their own specialized '''jargons''' and have a tendency to be difficult to penetrate.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[speech|Speech]] or language that is [[incomprehensible]] or [[unintelligible]]; [[gibberish]].

